=== layout: two-panels

# 18. xDNA and XNA

@left

![Slide image 55](assets/lect06c-set_4_tp_chapter_12_16_material/image_055.png)

@right

- Scientists have been investigating properties of so-called xenonucleic acids or XNAs.
- They have demonstrated for the first time that six of these unnatural nucleic acid polymers are capable of sharing information with DNA.
- One of these XNAs, a molecule referred to as anhydrohexitol nucleic acid or HNA, was capable of undergoing directed evolution and folding into biologically useful forms.

@notes

Source: ../old/lect06c-set_4_TP-chapter_12-16_material.odp, slide 54.
2026 topic 18: xDNA/XNA. Original content retained and regrouped.

